Ask three people in hospitality development to define "fit-out" versus "FF&E" and you will
often get three different answers. The distinction matters because it changes how a project
gets budgeted, tendered and sequenced.

## Fit-out is what makes the building a room

Fit-out covers everything fixed to the structure: partitions, ceilings, wall finishes,
millwork, wet areas. It is largely immovable and typically the first work to start once a
shell is handed over, because everything else depends on it being finished.

## FF&E is what makes the room a hotel

Furniture, fixtures and equipment is everything that arrives after the fit-out is
substantially complete — loose furniture, soft furnishings, artwork, decorative lighting,
casegoods. It is what a guest actually notices, and it is procured on a different timeline
because much of it is manufactured off-site while fit-out is still underway.

## Where the line blurs, and why it matters

Fixed furniture — a reception desk, a built-in wardrobe — sits in the gap between the two,
and different contracts draw the line in different places. Getting this agreed early, in the
contract rather than on site, is what prevents a scope dispute six months into a project.

The properties that open on time are the ones where fit-out and FF&E were planned as one
continuous programme from day one, not as two separate procurements that happen to meet in
the same room.
